---
// v1.1 session 5: search, via Pagefind (built at deploy time over the
// article content — see package.json's "postbuild" script and Article.
// astro/ReflectionArticle.astro's data-pagefind-body/-ignore attributes
// for what actually gets indexed). No client filtering of categories or
// tags here — those stay the static links already built in session 4;
// this is free-text search only.
//
// Pagefind's own JS (~a few hundred KB, scales with index size) is
// genuinely heavy, so it's never loaded on page load — only on the first
// focus or keystroke in this field, via a dynamic import(). The small
// wiring script below (which just knows *when* to trigger that import)
// always loads; the actual Pagefind runtime does not, until interaction.
//
// Session 6: the header search button links here with `?focus=search`
// rather than a plain `/articles` link — a query param, not a URL fragment,
// since a fragment alone wouldn't actually focus the field (browsers don't
// auto-focus a form element just because it matches an #id in the URL).
// The script below checks for that param on load and calls .focus()
// itself, which fires this same input's existing 'focus' listener below —
// so a header-search click lazy-loads Pagefind exactly the same way a
// direct click into the box already did, no separate code path.
//
// Post-session-5 fix, results rendering rewritten: the result rows were
// completely unstyled in the real browser despite this file's own <style>
// block defining .search__result and friends — traced to an Astro-specific
// gotcha, not a CSS mistake. Astro's scoped <style> works by stamping a
// build-time data-astro-cid-* attribute onto every element the compiler
// sees and rewriting that component's selectors to require it. The result
// rows are never compiled by Astro at all — they're plain strings built
// in this script and inserted via `results.innerHTML =` at runtime — so
// they never get that attribute, and the scoped selectors silently never
// matched them. A DOM/text-content check can't catch this (the elements
// and classes are all there, correctly), only actually rendering the page
// and looking at it can. All of the result-row styling now lives unscoped
// in global.css instead, the same fix already used for .email-capture and
// .reflection-inline-photo — both raw HTML injected outside Astro's
// compiler for their own, different reasons. Worth remembering for any
// future feature that builds markup via innerHTML: it needs unscoped CSS
// by construction, not as an afterthought if it turns out invisible.
//
// Also fixed in the same pass: results are built from Pagefind's own
// structured per-result data (title, excerpt, url, and now a `category`
// meta field — see data-pagefind-meta on Article.astro/
// ReflectionArticle.astro) into this component's own markup — never
// Pagefind's own default result UI, which this project has never used.
// One row per matching article was already true before this fix (Pagefind
// indexes one result per page, confirmed by inspecting real multi-result
// output in a prior session) — what was actually broken was purely the
// visual styling, not the underlying data or result count.
---

<div class="search">
  <label for="search-input" class="visually-hidden">Search articles</label>
  <input
    type="search"
    id="search-input"
    class="search__input"
    placeholder="Search articles"
    autocomplete="off"
    role="combobox"
    aria-expanded="false"
    aria-controls="search-results"
    aria-autocomplete="list"
  />
  <div id="search-results" class="search__results" role="listbox" hidden></div>
</div>

{/*
  Unminified for reference:

    (function () {
      var input = document.getElementById('search-input');
      var results = document.getElementById('search-results');
      if (!input || !results) return;

      var pagefindPromise;
      function loadPagefind() {
        if (!pagefindPromise) {
          // Fails harmlessly in `astro dev`/before a build has run
          // Pagefind's own indexing step — there's no /pagefind/ output
          // until then.
          pagefindPromise = import('/pagefind/pagefind.js')
            .then(function (mod) {
              return mod.init().then(function () {
                return mod;
              });
            })
            .catch(function () {
              return null;
            });
        }
        return pagefindPromise;
      }

      // Current result set + which one (if any) is keyboard-highlighted —
      // tracked here rather than read back from the DOM, since the DOM
      // gets fully rebuilt on every render.
      var items = [];
      var activeIndex = -1;

      function clear() {
        results.innerHTML = '';
        results.hidden = true;
        results.classList.remove('is-visible');
        input.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false');
        input.removeAttribute('aria-activedescendant');
        items = [];
        activeIndex = -1;
      }

      function show() {
        results.hidden = false;
        input.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'true');
        // Next frame, so the opacity transition actually runs from the
        // hidden state instead of jumping straight to visible.
        requestAnimationFrame(function () {
          results.classList.add('is-visible');
        });
      }

      // One clean row per article: a pillar-colored category label (same
      // .category-pill/.pillar--<category> classes an article's own title
      // block uses), the real title, and Pagefind's own short excerpt
      // (already truncated to a relevant snippet with <mark> around the
      // match — CSS line-clamps it to 2 lines as a second, visual backstop).
      // role="option"/id pairs with the input's aria-activedescendant below
      // for accessible keyboard highlighting without moving real DOM focus
      // off the text field.
      function renderItems() {
        if (items.length === 0) {
          results.innerHTML = '<p class="search__empty">No results.</p>';
          return;
        }
        results.innerHTML = items
          .map(function (item, i) {
